          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            In
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra"} algebra
            %em Wedderburn-Artin theorem
            \&amp;lbrack;
            %a{:href => "#Wedderburn1908"}> Wedderburn 1908
            ,
            = succeed "&rbrack;" do
              %a{:href => "#Artin27"} Artin 1927
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rings"} rings
            that are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/matrix+algebras"} matrix algebras
            over
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/division+rings"} division rings
          %p
            Concretely, the theorem says that any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semisimple+ring"} semisimple ring
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+limit"} finite
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/direct+sum"} direct sum
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/matrix+algebras"} matrix algebras
            over
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/division+rings"} division rings
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ring"} ring
            is called
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semisimple+ring"} semisimple
            if each of its left or equivalently right
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modules"} modules
            is a finite
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/direct+sum"} direct sum
            of
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simple+modules"} simple modules
          %h2#statement Statement
          %p
          #TheTheorem.num_prop
            %h6 Proposition
            %p
              %strong (Wedderburn–Artin Theorem)
              %br/
              Every
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semisimple+ring"} semisimple ring
              is
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphic
              to a finite
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/direct+sum"} direct sum
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/matrix+algebras"} matrix algebras
              over
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/division+rings"} division rings
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simple+ring"} simple
              if and only if it is a matrix algebra over a division ring.
          %p
          .num_remark
            %h6 Remark
            %p
              Beware: A
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simple+ring"} simple ring
              may not be semisimple! A ring is simple iff it has no two-sided ideals except
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{0\}
              and the whole ring, and in the absence of further hypotheses this does not imply that all of its left (or equivalently right) modules are direct sums of simple modules. For example, the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Weyl+algebra"} Weyl algebra
              is simple but not semisimple, and not isomorphic to a matrix algebra over a division ring.
              %br/
          %p
            However, a simple ring that is left or right
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Artinian+ring"} artinian
            is semisimple, so we have:
          %p
          #Corollary.num_cor
            %h6 Corollary
            %p
              %br/
              Every
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simple+ring"} simple ring
              that is left or right artinian is
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphic
              to a matrix algebra over a division algebra.
          %p
            There is also a version of the Wedderburn–Artin theorem for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associative+algebras"} associative algebras
            over
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fields"} fields
          %p
          .num_prop
            %h6 Proposition
            %p
              %strong (Wedderburn–Artin Theorem for Algebras over Fields)
              %br/
              Every
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semisimple+algebra"} semisimple algebra
              over a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field"} field
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
              is
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphic
              to a finite
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/direct+sum"} direct sum
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/matrix+algebras"} matrix algebras
              over
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/division+algebras"} division algebras
              over
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
              is simple if and only if it is a matrix algebra over a division algebra over
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
          %p
            There is also a version for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/endomorphism+rigs"} endomorphism rigs
            in a
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semiadditive+category"} semiadditive category
          %p
          .num_prop
            %h6 Proposition
            %p
              %strong (Wedderburn–Artin Theorem for Endomorphism rigs)
              %br/
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semisimple+object"} semisimple object
              in a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semiadditive+category"} semiadditive category
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "sans-serif"}
                        %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathsf{A}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/endomorphism+rig"} endomorphism rig
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi End
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi R
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} End(R)
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+product"} finite product
              of matrix rigs over
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/division+rigs"} division rigs
          %h2#proofs Proofs
          %p
            There are many
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proofs"} proofs
            of the Wedderburn–Artin theorem (Prop.
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#TheTheorem"}
          %h3#common_proof Common proof
          %p A common modern approach proceeds as follows.
          %p
            Suppose
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            is semisimple. Then by definition any right
            = succeed "-module" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simple+module"}
                simple
                = succeed "-modules" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi R
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            = succeed "-module" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            = succeed "-module" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi R
                    %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R_R
            is isomorphic to a finite direct sum of simple
            = succeed "-modules," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/minimal+ideal"} minimal
            right
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ideals"} ideals
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi R
                    %mi R
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ≅
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⨁
                    %mi i
                  %msubsup
                    %mi I
                    %mi i
                    %mrow
                      %mo ⊕
                      %msub
                        %mi m
                        %mi i
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  R_R
                  \\;\cong\;
                  \\bigoplus_i I_i^{\oplus m_i}
                  \\,,
          %p
            where the
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi I
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I_i
            are mutually nonisomorphic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simple+module"} simple
            right
            = succeed "-modules," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            = succeed "th" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi m
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m_i
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/endomorphisms"} endomorphisms
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi End
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi R
                    %mi R
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ≅
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⨁
                    %mi i
                  %mi End
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                  %msubsup
                    %mi I
                    %mi i
                    %mrow
                      %mo ⊕
                      %msub
                        %mi m
                        %mi i
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  End(R_R)
                  \\;\cong\;
                  \\bigoplus_i End\big(I_i^{\oplus m_i}\big)
          %p
            and we can identify
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi End
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                  %msubsup
                    %mi I
                    %mi i
                    %mrow
                      %mo ⊕
                      %msub
                        %mi m
                        %mi i
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} End\big(I_i^{\oplus m_i}\big)
            with a ring of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/matrices"} matrices
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi End
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                  %msubsup
                    %mi I
                    %mi i
                    %mrow
                      %mo ⊕
                      %msub
                        %mi m
                        %mi i
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ≅
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %msub
                    %mi M
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi m
                        %mi i
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                  %mi End
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi I
                    %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  End\big(I_i^{\oplus m_i}\big)
                  \\;\cong\;
                  M_{m_i}\big(End(I_i)\big)
                  \\,,
          %p
            where the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/endomorphism+ring"} endomorphism ring
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi End
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi I
                    %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} End(I_i)
            of the right
            = succeed "-module" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi I
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I_i
            is a division ring by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Schur%27s+lemma"} Schur's lemma
            because
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi I
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I_i
            is simple. Since
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                  %mo ≅
                  %mi End
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi R
                    %mi R
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R \cong End(R_R)
            we conclude
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ≅
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⨁
                    %mi i
                  %msub
                    %mi M
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi m
                        %mi i
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                  %mi End
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi I
                    %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  R \;\cong\;
                  \\bigoplus_i M_{m_i}\big(End(I_i)\big)
                  \\,.
          %p
            (We use right modules because
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                    %mo ≅
                    %mi End
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi R
                      %mi R
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R \cong End(R_R)
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                    %mo ≅
                    %mi End
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mrow
                      %mi R
                    %mi R
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi op
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R \cong End({}_R R)^{op}
          %h3#nicholsons_proof Nicholson’s proof
          %p
            A different style of proof can be found in
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Nicholson93"} Nicholson (1993)
          %p
          #BrauerLemma.num_lemma
            %h6 Lemma
            %p
              %strong (Brauer’s Lemma)
              %br/
              Suppose
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
              is a ring and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                    %mo ⊆
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K \subseteq R
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/minimal+ideal"} minimal left ideal
              with
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi K
                        %mn 2
                      %mo ≠
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K^2 \ne 0
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                    %mo =
                    %mi R
                    %mi e
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K = R e
              for some
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi e
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi K
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e \in K
              with
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi e
                        %mn 2
                      %mo =
                      %mi e
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e^2 = e
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi e
                    %mi R
                    %mi e
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e R e
              is a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/division+ring"} division ring
          %p
            Here a
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/minimal+ideal"} minimal left ideal
            is a nonzero left ideal for which the only smaller left ideal is
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{0\}
          %p
            For example, suppose
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            is the ring of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mo ×
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \times n
            matrices over some division ring
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            This has a minimal ideal
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            consisting of matrices with just one nonzero column, say the
            = succeed "th" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi j
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} j
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi K
                      %mn 2
                    %mo ≠
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K^2 \ne 0
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                    %mo =
                    %mi R
                    %mi e
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K = R e
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi e
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e
            to be the matrix that’s zero everywhere except for a 1 in the
            = succeed "th" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi j
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} j
            = succeed "th" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi j
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} j
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi e
                      %mn 2
                    %mo =
                    %mi e
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e^2 = e
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi e
                  %mi R
                  %mi e
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e R e
            consists of matrices that are zero except in the
            = succeed "th" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi j
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} j
            = succeed "th" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi j
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} j
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi e
                  %mi R
                  %mi e
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e R e
            is isomorphic to
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
          %p No deep techniques or preliminary lemmas are required to prove Brauer’s Lemma.
          %p
            %strong
              Proof of Brauer’s Lemma (
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#BrauerLemma"}>
              )
            = succeed "Since" do
              %br/
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ≠
                    %msup
                      %mi K
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 \ne K^2
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                  %mi u
                  %mo ≠
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K u \ne 0
            for some
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi u
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi K
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u \in K
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi u
                    %mo ≠
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u \ne 0
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                  %mi u
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K u
            is a left ideal contained in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                  %mi u
                  %mo =
                  %mi K
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K u = K.
          %p
            Thus
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi u
                  %mo =
                  %mi e
                  %mi u
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u = e u
            for some
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi e
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi K
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e \in K
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi a
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi K
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi a
                  %mi u
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L = \{a \in K \colon a u = 0 \}
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
            is a left ideal since for any
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi r
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r \in R
            we have
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi L
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ⇒
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi a
                  %mi u
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ⇒
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi r
                  %mi a
                  %mi u
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ⇒
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi r
                  %mi a
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi L
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  a \in L
                  \\; \implies \;
                  a u = 0
                  \\; \implies \;
                  r a u  = 0
                  \\; \implies \;
                  r a \in L
                  \\,.
          %p
            Note
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mi K
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L \subseteq K
            by definition, so by the minimality of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            we must have either
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L = 0
            or
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                    %mo =
                    %mi K
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L = K
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi e
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e
            is in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            but not in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi e
                    %mi u
                    %mo =
                    %mi u
                    %mo ≠
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e u = u \ne 0
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L = 0
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi K
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mtext and
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi a
                  %mi u
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                  %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                  %mo ⇒
                  %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                  %mi a
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  a \in K
                  \\; \text{and} \;
                  a u = 0
                  \\quad \implies \quad
                  a = 0
                  \\,.
          %p
            To use this implication, recall that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi u
                  %mo =
                  %mi e
                  %mi u
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u = e u
            so
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi e
                  %mi u
                  %mo =
                  %msup
                    %mi e
                    %mn 2
                  %mi u
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e u = e^2 u
            so
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi e
                    %mo −
                    %msup
                      %mi e
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi u
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (e - e^2)u = 0
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
            above to be
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi e
                    %mo −
                    %msup
                      %mi e
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e - e^2
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi e
                    %mo −
                    %msup
                      %mi e
                      %mn 2
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e - e^2 = 0
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi e
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e
            is
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/idempotent"} idempotent
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi e
                    %mn 2
                  %mo =
                  %mi e
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e^2 = e \,.
          %p
            Now we claim that
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                    %mo =
                    %mi R
                    %mi e
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K = R e
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi e
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e
            is in the left ideal
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                    %mi e
                    %mo ⊆
                    %mi K
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R e \subseteq K
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            is minimal this implies either
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                  %mi e
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R e = 0
            or
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                    %mi e
                    %mo =
                    %mi K
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R e = K
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi e
                  %mo ≠
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e \ne 0
            so
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                    %mi e
                    %mo ≠
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R e \ne 0
          %p
            Why is
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi e
                  %mi R
                  %mi e
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e R e
            a division ring? Its unit is
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi e
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi b
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi e
                  %mi R
                  %mi e
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b \in e R e
            is nonzero. To show
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b
            has an inverse, note that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R b
            is a nonzero ideal contained in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                  %mi e
                  %mo =
                  %mi K
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R e = K
            so by minimality
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                    %mi b
                    %mo =
                    %mi R
                    %mi e
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R b = R e
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi e
                  %mo =
                  %mi r
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e = r b
            for some
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi r
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r \in R
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi e
                  %mi r
                  %mi e
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e r e
            is the left inverse of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b
            in
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi e
                    %mi R
                    %mi e
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e R e
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi e
                  %mi r
                  %mi e
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi b
                  %mo =
                  %mi e
                  %mi r
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi e
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi e
                  %mi r
                  %mi b
                  %mo =
                  %msup
                    %mi e
                    %mn 2
                  %mo =
                  %mi e
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (e r e) b = e r (e b) = e r b = e^2 = e.
          %p
            Finally, in a ring where every nonzero element has a
            %em left
            inverse, every element has a two-sided inverse, so it’s a division ring! To see this, say
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi b
                    %mo ≠
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b \ne 0
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            for short. To see that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            also the right inverse of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b
            note that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            must also have its own left inverse, say
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mi b
                  %mo =
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x b = 1
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi y
                    %mi x
                    %mo =
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y x = 1
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi y
                  %mo =
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi y
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi b
                  %mo =
                  %mi b
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y = y (x b) = (y x) b = b .
          %p
            Thus
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b
            is the left inverse of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            is the right inverse of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/central+simple+algebra"} central simple algebra
          %h2#references References
          %p The original articles:
          %ul
            %li#Wedderburn1908
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Joseph+H.+M.+Wedderburn"}> Joseph H. M. Wedderburn
                \:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em On hypercomplex numbers
                %strong s2-6
                1 (1908) 77-118 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1112/plms/s2-6.1.77"}> doi:10.1112/plms/s2-6.1.77
                \&amp;rbrack;
            %li#Artin27
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Emil+Artin"}> Emil Artin
                \:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Zur Theorie der hyperkomplexen Zahlen
                %strong 5
                (1927) 251–260 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/BF02952526"}> doi:10.1007/BF02952526
                \&amp;rbrack;
          %p Review:
          %ul
            %li#Nicholson93
              %p
                William K. Nicholson,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em A short proof of the Wedderburn–Artin theorem
                %em New Zealand J. Math
                %strong 22
                (1993) 83-86 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://www.thebookshelf.auckland.ac.nz/docs/NZJMaths/nzjmaths022/nzjmaths022-01-010.pdf"}> pdf
                \&amp;rbrack;
            %li
              %p
                Tsiu-Kwen Lee,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em A short proof of the Wedderburn-Artin theorem
